# Extra articles (converted from long videos). Each: dict(slug,title,desc,date,tagline,cat,body[,draft])
# draft=True pages are not built until released.
def vid(url, label="Watch the video version on YouTube"):
    return f'<div class="box"><b class="t">Prefer video?</b><a href="{url}" rel="noopener">{label}</a></div>'

MORE = []

MORE.append(dict(
 slug="fasted-morning-walk-fat-loss", date="2026-11-21", cat="Fat loss",
 title="Fasted Morning Walk: Does It Really Help Burn Belly Fat?",
 desc="What a fasted morning walk does to insulin, growth hormone and fat burning, what the research really says, and the simple routine I follow.",
 tagline="Insulin, growth hormone and fuel: why the timing of your walk matters, and an honest look at the evidence.",
 body=vid("https://youtu.be/Y_MXwb9Qnio") + """
<p class="lead">You walk almost every day. You have cut back on junk food. Yet the belly fat will not move. Often the issue is not how much you walk, but when. Here is what a fasted morning walk does, what the research does and does not say, and how I do it.</p>
<h2>Reason 1: insulin is the lock on your fat stores</h2>
<p>Every time you eat, especially carbs, insulin rises. It moves sugar from your food into your muscles, liver and brain, which is a good thing. But while insulin is high, your body is in storage mode and releases very little fat. It works like a dimmer, not a switch: the higher insulin sits, the less fat you release.</p>
<p>If you eat within the hour of waking, snack mid-morning, have lunch, evening snacks and dinner, insulin barely comes down all day. First thing in the morning, after seven or eight hours without food, it is at its lowest point. Walk then, and your muscles reach for stored fat because no new food is coming in.</p>
<h2>Reason 2: growth hormone and fat release</h2>
<p>Growth hormone is one of the body's signals to pull fat out of storage. Insulin and growth hormone tend to move in opposite directions: when insulin is high, growth hormone stays low. A fasted morning gives growth hormone more room to work.</p>
<p>Pace matters too. At an easy walking pace, fat supplies a big share of the energy you burn. Push much harder and your body switches to faster fuel, which is sugar. You do not need to run. An easy, comfortable walk of about five to six kilometres an hour is the sweet spot.</p>
<h2>Reason 3: training your body to use fat</h2>
<p>A body fed carbs all day gets very good at burning sugar and out of practice at burning fat. Regular fasted training can improve how well your muscles use fat for fuel, often called metabolic flexibility. Many people notice steadier energy and less mid-morning hunger after a few weeks.</p>
<h2>An honest note on the evidence</h2>
<p>Studies that compare exercise before and after breakfast often find more fat burned during the fasted session. Over weeks, though, total fat loss is mainly decided by your overall calorie intake. If you walk fasted and then overeat at lunch and dinner, the locker simply fills up again. Fasted walking does not replace a calorie deficit. It can make the deficit easier to hold.</p>
<h2>My routine</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Prepare the night before.</b> Clothes and shoes ready, no excuses.</li>
<li><b>Walk within 30 minutes of waking, on an empty stomach.</b> Warm water, lime water or green tea is fine. I skip black coffee, milk tea and biscuits before the walk.</li>
<li><b>Go outdoors, 25 to 30 minutes, at an easy pace.</b> Morning light also helps your body clock, which supports better sleep.</li>
<li><b>Once it feels easy, try interval walking:</b> 5 minutes warm-up, then 3 minutes fast and 3 minutes easy, repeated.</li>
<li><b>Eat a protein-rich breakfast afterwards,</b> around 30 grams of protein: eggs, Greek yogurt or whey.</li>
<li><b>Aim for 8,000 steps a day,</b> and a short walk after each meal makes it easy.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consistency beats intensity. This is a daily habit, not a 30-day challenge.</p>
<div class="box"><b class="t">Check first if</b>you have diabetes, take blood sugar medication, are pregnant, or have any medical condition. Ask your doctor before training on an empty stomach.</div>
"""))

MORE.append(dict(
 slug="visceral-fat-belly-fat-explained", date="2026-11-21", cat="Fat loss",
 title="Visceral Fat: Why Belly Fat Is Not All the Same, and How to Lose It",
 desc="What visceral fat is, why it matters for your health, why it responds first when you exercise, and a simple waist check you can do at home.",
 tagline="The fat you cannot pinch, why it matters, and the habits that shrink it first.",
 body=vid("https://youtu.be/qOCKwFrnxfw") + """
<p class="lead">You can have slim arms and a normal BMI and still carry fat deep inside your belly. You cannot pinch it or see it in the mirror. It is called visceral fat, and the good news is that it is also one of the first fats to go when you change your habits.</p>
<h2>Two kinds of belly fat</h2>
<p><b>Subcutaneous fat</b> sits just under the skin. It is the soft fat you can pinch, like a backpack carried on the outside. <b>Visceral fat</b> sits deep inside, behind your stomach muscles and around your organs. A belly that feels firm when you press it can be visceral fat pushing the stomach wall out from the inside.</p>
<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p><b>It drains into the liver.</b> Visceral fat releases fat through a direct route to the liver. Over time that can contribute to fatty liver, which is common even in people who never drink.</p>
<p><b>It is metabolically active.</b> Visceral fat sends out inflammatory signals. Higher levels are linked with raised blood sugar, blood pressure and heart disease risk. That is why doctors increasingly look at your waist, not just your weight.</p>
<p><b>It can lock itself in.</b> Two hormones play a part. Insulin stays high when you snack all day and drink sugary drinks, which keeps fat locked in. Cortisol, the stress hormone, stays high with long work hours, stress and short sleep, and visceral fat is especially sensitive to it. That is why many busy professionals stay slim everywhere except the belly.</p>
<h2>The good news: it responds first</h2>
<p>Think of subcutaneous fat as a fixed deposit and visceral fat as cash in your wallet. It is active and easy for the body to spend. A 2016 review in Obesity Reviews found that exercise reduces visceral fat even when body weight barely changes. So the scale can look the same while you lose the fat that matters most.</p>
<p>What does not work is spot reduction. In one study, people did ab exercises daily for six weeks. Their abs got stronger, but their belly fat did not change. Fat comes off the whole body, and visceral fat tends to respond first.</p>
<h2>A simple waist check</h2>
<p>Measure your height with a piece of string and fold it in half. Wrap it around your waist, midway between the lowest rib and the top of your hip bone. If the ends meet, your waist is less than half your height. Health guidelines (such as NICE in the UK) use this waist-to-height ratio as a warning sign. It is not a diagnosis. Only a body scan can measure visceral fat exactly, but this check is a better signal than weight or BMI alone. Do it once a month.</p>
<h2>What I do</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Walk every day:</b> a fasted morning walk, 8,000 steps, and short walks after meals.</li>
<li><b>Lift weights at least four days a week.</b> More muscle gives sugar more places to go.</li>
<li><b>Protein and fibre at every meal.</b> Chicken or fish, eggs, Greek yogurt and plenty of vegetables. One study linked 10 extra grams of soluble fibre a day to less visceral fat over five years.</li>
<li><b>Cut sugary drinks.</b> Liquid sugar goes almost straight to the liver.</li>
<li><b>Sleep seven to eight hours and manage stress.</b> That is how you ease the cortisol lock.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul><li>Verheggen et al., Obesity Reviews, 2016: exercise and visceral fat.</li><li>Vispute et al., Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, 2011: ab training and belly fat.</li><li>Hairston et al., Obesity, 2012: soluble fibre and visceral fat.</li><li>Ashwell et al., Obesity Reviews, 2012: waist-to-height ratio.</li><li>NICE guideline, 2022.</li></ul>
<div class="box"><b class="t">Check first if</b>you have a liver condition, diabetes or any medical condition. Talk to your doctor before making big changes.</div>
"""))

MORE.append(dict(
 slug="eat-in-the-right-order-blood-sugar", date="2026-11-21", cat="Nutrition",
 title="Eat in the Right Order: How Carbs Last Can Lower Your Sugar Spike",
 desc="Why eating vegetables and protein before carbs can lower the blood sugar spike after a meal, what the studies found, and how to do it at an Indian meal.",
 tagline="Same food, smarter order: what research shows about vegetables, protein and carbs.",
 body=vid("https://youtu.be/L5uAnuvuu7I") + """
<p class="lead">Two people eat the same lunch: the same rice, chicken and salad, with the same calories. One gets a big sugar spike and a crash an hour later. The other barely spikes. The difference can be the order they ate it in.</p>
<h2>What the research found</h2>
<p>In a study from Weill Cornell Medicine, 16 adults with type 2 diabetes ate the same meal on three different days: carbs first, carbs last, or carbs split in the middle. When carbs came last, the blood sugar peak was about 54% lower than with carbs first, and about 25% less insulin was needed. Those volunteers had type 2 diabetes, where the effect is strongest. Later studies in prediabetes and in healthy young adults saw the same direction, though the effect is smaller when blood sugar is already healthy.</p>
<h2>What a sugar spike is</h2>
<p>Carbs break down into glucose, which enters your blood. Your pancreas releases insulin to clear it. A small rise is normal. A big spike means a big wave of insulin, and while insulin is high, fat burning is switched off. The goal is not zero carbs. It is a gentle hill instead of a mountain.</p>
<h2>Two built-in brakes</h2>
<p><b>The fibre net.</b> Vegetables eaten first bring fibre that forms a gel in your gut, so the sugar from your carbs soaks through slowly. Fibre also takes up room, so you tend to eat a little less rice.</p>
<p><b>Protein and GLP-1.</b> Protein triggers your gut to release GLP-1, a hormone that slows the stomach's exit gate and tells your brain you are getting full. A Japanese study found that fish or meat before rice slowed stomach emptying, raised GLP-1, and lowered the sugar rise after the meal.</p>
<h2>The crash that makes you snack</h2>
<p>What goes up fast can come down fast. After a big spike, blood sugar can dip below where it started two or three hours later, which makes you sleepy and pulls you towards biscuits and sugary coffee. In the large PREDICT study, people with the biggest dips felt hungry sooner and ate around 300 more calories over the day.</p>
<h2>How I build every plate</h2>
<ol>
<li><b>Vegetables first.</b> A salad, a clear vegetable soup, or the vegetables on your plate.</li>
<li><b>Protein next.</b> Chicken, fish or eggs. Vegetarian options: paneer, tofu, soya chunks, Greek yogurt or chickpeas.</li>
<li><b>Carbs last.</b> Rice, roti, bread or pasta. You do not need a timer. Just eat slowly.</li>
</ol>
<p>At home, carbs usually come mixed with a curry or side dish. An easy fix: eat about 70% of your protein first and keep the last 30% to enjoy with your rice or roti. The meal still tastes like home.</p>
<h2>Honest limits</h2>
<p>This does not make carbs free. A mountain of rice in the right order is still a mountain of rice. Fat loss still needs a modest calorie deficit, and the easiest route is building meals around protein and vegetables.</p>
<h2>Habits that help the order work</h2>
<ul>
<li>Move after the meal: a 10-minute walk, or three sets of 15 bodyweight squats. Even 2 to 5 minutes of light movement softens the spike.</li>
<li>Lift weights four or more days a week. Muscle is your biggest storage tank for sugar.</li>
<li>Aim for 8,000 to 10,000 steps a day.</li>
<li>Skip sugary drinks and watch hidden sugar in tea, biscuits, packaged snacks, cereals and sauces.</li>
<li>Eat dinner early, three to four hours before sleep. Even one short night can make your body handle the next breakfast worse.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul><li>Shukla et al., BMJ Open Diabetes Research and Care, 2017 and 2019; Diabetes Care, 2015.</li><li>Imai et al., Nutrients, 2023.</li><li>Kuwata et al., Diabetologia, 2016.</li><li>Wyatt et al., Nature Metabolism, 2021 (PREDICT).</li><li>Buffey et al., Sports Medicine, 2022.</li><li>Donga et al., Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism, 2010.</li></ul>
<div class="box"><b class="t">Check first if</b>you have diabetes or take medicine for blood sugar. This can change your numbers, so talk to your doctor first.</div>
"""))
